\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

\subsection{Models}
\label{sec:models}

We conduct experiments on two 7B-parameter instruction-tuned models that share the Qwen2 backbone architecture (28 Transformer layers, 28 attention heads with 4-group GQA):

\paragraph{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B} serves as our primary evaluation target. This model was distilled from DeepSeek-R1 \cite{deepseek-r1} and produces explicit chain-of-thought traces delimited by \texttt{<think>}$\cdots$\texttt{</think>} tags, yielding sequences with pronounced phase structure---extended reasoning followed by a concise solution. The structured generation pattern makes it well-suited for studying how sparsity interacts with distinct reasoning phases.

\paragraph{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} \cite{qwen2.5} provides a cross-model replication. Although it shares the same backbone family, its instruction tuning does not enforce explicit reasoning delimiters, producing a different distribution of attention sparsity across the generation trajectory. Replication on this model tests whether our findings generalize beyond distillation-induced phase structure.

Both models are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} precision with eager attention (i.e., without FlashAttention) to permit direct manipulation of attention weight matrices during sparsity intervention.\footnote{An earlier \texttt{float16} configuration produced sporadic NaN values in Layer~27 of DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, affecting ${\sim}$8\% of logit positions. Switching to \texttt{bfloat16} eliminated all NaN occurrences; we additionally apply NaN-guarded filtering as a safety net.}


\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We evaluate on a 50-problem stratified subset of the MATH-500 benchmark \cite{math500}, sampling uniformly across problem categories (algebra, geometry, number theory, combinatorics, etc.) with a fixed seed for reproducibility. Mathematical reasoning tasks are a natural testbed for our study: they elicit long chain-of-thought sequences (up to 2048 tokens) in which the model alternates between exploratory reasoning and structured computation---phases that differ markedly in attention sparsity. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048) to eliminate sampling variance.


\subsection{Compound Sparsity Configuration}
\label{sec:sparsity-config}

We apply \emph{symmetric} compound sparsity: for a given sparsity level $s$, we prune the same fraction $s$ of attention heads and evict the same fraction $s$ of KV cache entries. We evaluate four sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, spanning from mild to aggressive compression.

\paragraph{Sparsity conditions.} For each problem and each sparsity level, we compute perplexity under four conditions: (i)~\emph{dense}---no intervention; (ii)~\emph{attn-only}---head pruning without KV eviction; (iii)~\emph{kv-only}---KV eviction without head pruning; and (iv)~\emph{both}---simultaneous head pruning and KV eviction.

\paragraph{Eviction strategies.} We compare two KV cache eviction strategies:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Importance-based.} Each KV position receives a score equal to its cumulative attention weight summed over all query positions and heads. To correct for the inherent causal bias---earlier positions accumulate more attention simply because they are attended to by more queries---we apply position normalization:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:importance}
    \alpha_j = \frac{\sum_{i,h} a_{i,h,j}}{\texttt{seq\_len} - j}, \quad j \in \{1, \dots, T\},
\end{equation}
where $a_{i,h,j}$ is the attention weight from query~$i$, head~$h$ to key~$j$, and the denominator counts the number of queries that can attend to position~$j$ under causal masking. Position~0 (BOS) is always retained. The $T \cdot s$ positions with the lowest normalized scores are evicted.

During masked forward passes, we guard against numerical instability from the interaction of head masking and KV eviction: zeroed-out heads that would otherwise produce $\infty \times 0 = \text{NaN}$ are handled via selective replacement (\texttt{torch.where}) prior to the softmax, and any layer producing NaN outputs is skipped from the sparsity loss computation.

\item \textbf{Random.} KV positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random, providing a strategy-agnostic baseline. Head pruning under the random condition selects heads via \texttt{torch.randperm} rather than by importance.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Head pruning.} Under importance-based eviction, heads are ranked by their Frobenius norm across the full attention matrix $\mathbf{A}_\ell \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times T \times T}$ per layer $\ell$, and the bottom-$s$ fraction is masked. Under random eviction, heads are selected uniformly at random.


\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:phase-seg}

We segment each generated sequence into three \emph{reasoning phases} based on the local attention sparsity of each token position. For each query position $i$, we compute per-head sparsity as the fraction of attention weights falling below $0.01 \times \max_j a_{i,h,j}$, then average across heads to obtain a scalar sparsity value per position. The resulting sparsity trajectory is partitioned into terciles via quantile boundaries: \emph{low-sparsity} (bottom third, corresponding to diffuse attention during exploratory reasoning), \emph{mid-sparsity}, and \emph{high-sparsity} (top third, corresponding to focused attention during structured computation). Contiguous segments shorter than $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}=10$ tokens are merged into adjacent phases to suppress noise.


\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Perplexity.} For each sparsity condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ and each phase, we compute segment-level perplexity as
$\text{PPL}_c = \exp\!\bigl(\tfrac{1}{|I|}\sum_{i \in I} \mathcal{L}_c(x_i)\bigr)$,
where $I$ is the set of token indices belonging to the phase and $\mathcal{L}_c(x_i)$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss under condition $c$.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.} The interaction index quantifies the synergistic (super-additive) effect of compound sparsity beyond the sum of individual interventions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ii}
    \mathrm{II} = \frac{\text{PPL}_b}{\text{PPL}_d} - \frac{\text{PPL}_a}{\text{PPL}_d} - \frac{\text{PPL}_k}{\text{PPL}_d} + 1.
\end{equation}
When $\mathrm{II} > 0$, the two sparsity mechanisms amplify each other; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive (independent) degradation.

\paragraph{Severity Gradient.} To measure how the interaction varies across reasoning phases, we define the severity gradient:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gradient}
    G = \frac{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}},
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}$ and $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}$ are the median interaction indices over the low-sparsity and high-sparsity phases, respectively. We report medians rather than means because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to 43:1 at $s{=}0.30$), making the median a more robust central tendency estimator.


\subsection{Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:stats}

All confidence intervals and significance tests are computed via the nonparametric bootstrap with $B = 10{,}000$ resamples \cite{efron1993bootstrap}.

\paragraph{Per-problem bootstrap.} For each sparsity level and strategy, we resample the 50 per-problem II values with replacement and compute the mean of each resample, yielding a 95\% percentile confidence interval for the population mean II.

\paragraph{Paired bootstrap.} To test whether the severity gradient differs between importance-based and random eviction, we pair observations by problem (same problem, same sparsity level) and bootstrap the difference in gradients. This controls for problem-level variance in difficulty and sequence length.

\paragraph{Unpaired bootstrap.} We additionally compute per-strategy gradient confidence intervals without pairing, to verify that the importance-based gradient is significantly greater than 1 (i.e., phase modulation exists) independently of the random baseline.

All tests yield $p < 0.0001$ at every sparsity level, confirming that the observed phase--strategy interactions are not artifacts of sampling variability.\footnote{Experiments were conducted on a server with 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition 96\,GB GPUs, with one model--sparsity configuration per GPU.}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present our empirical findings in five parts: strategy-dependent compound severity (\S\ref{sec:strategy-severity}), phase-modulated amplification (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}), the phase-strategy crossover (\S\ref{sec:crossover}), cross-model replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), and statistical validation (\S\ref{sec:stat-validation}).


\subsection{Experimental Overview}
\label{sec:exp-overview}

We evaluate compound sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B using a 50-problem stratified subset of MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:dataset}). For each problem, we measure perplexity under four conditions---dense, attention-only pruning, KV-only eviction, and compound (both)---at sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. We compare importance-based and random eviction strategies (\S\ref{sec:sparsity-config}), and segment each generated sequence into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity reasoning phases via quantile-based attention sparsity partitioning (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). The interaction index (II, Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) quantifies compound super-additivity, and the severity gradient ($G$, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) captures phase-dependent amplification.
Models are evaluated in \texttt{bfloat16} precision (\S\ref{sec:experimental-setup}). Full experimental details appear therein.


\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Severity (C1)}
\label{sec:strategy-severity}

The aggregate interaction index reveals a stark dependence on eviction strategy. Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii} reports the problem-level mean II across all 50 problems for each strategy and sparsity level. Under importance-based eviction, compound sparsity produces substantial super-additive degradation that grows with sparsity: mean II increases from \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s015} at $s{=}0.15$ to \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_importance, metric: mean_II_s030} at $s{=}0.30$. Under random eviction, compound interaction shows no systematic phase modulation across sparsity levels, with mean II near \placeholder{exp_id: deepseek_bf16_random, metric: mean_II_range}.

